            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



Overall, your house is very leaky from an air leakage perspective, and I have identified a number of locations that can be addressed:



** The largest source is through the wall that has been knocked down in the main room of the home, resulting in a lot of cold air coming from the attic space above. Once this is fixed your air leakage will be much improved.



** Another large source of air leakage is the open combustion air pipe (5" in diameter) located beside the boiler in your mechanical room under the main floor of the home - this pipe is likely a significant cause of this room being cold. With a furnace upgrade to a new condensing furnace it would eliminate the combustion air pipe.



** Both doors are leaky around the frame, and through the weather stripping. Replacement should be considered at this time. When replacement occurrs, ensure that spray foam is applied between the entire outside edge of the door frame and the walls.



** Some windows are leaky around the frames, or do not latch well, and should be addressed with a full frame window replacement. See Window upgrade section for more details.



** Around the attic hatch is a small source of air leakage upstairs. This can be sealed with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack".



** The main bathroom exhaust fan on the upper floor was a bit leaky when the home was depressurized. Bathroom fans can be replaced with energy star rated fans with butterfly style dampers, that seal better than traditional exhaust fan dampers.



** The header spaces in the main floor of your home (visible from the utility area) are insulated with fibreglass batt insulation, which is good from an insulation perspective, but is doing nothing to stop air leakage at the headers. Consider replacing fibreglass batt insulation with a spray foam application.



** Around the outside edge of the crawl space door. Add weather stripping to the door.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate you could reduce your air leakage by 30% or more. For modelling purposes, I estimated a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, which is estimated to save you $310 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 8.1 GJ. These savings are acheivable for relatively low effort/ expense.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main exterior walls of your home, for the purposes of our modelling, appear to be of 2x4 construction, at 16 inches on center, with R12 batts in the cavities, and 1.5 inches of extruded polystyrene on the outside (R7.5). This level of insulation is okay for your climate, but ideally would be better.



Current building practices suggest a minimum of R30 wall insulation for this climate. I've included a wall insulation upgrade in this report to give you an idea of what your savings would be if you were to add an additional 2 inches (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to 100% of your wall exterioir surfaces. Due to the large amount of labour involved in re-insulating  a home - and the high cost - this recommendation should only be taken if you choose to complete a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 2 additional inches (R10) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 430 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11 GJ, and qualify for up to a $1,500 rebate (if insulating 100% of the surface area) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. Please note that the rebate amount depends on the percentage of surface area re-insulated, and the R value of insulation added. Additionally, a minimum of 20% of the surface area must be insulated to qualify. If this were something you were interested in doing it would be best to discuss it over the phone in more detail.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate for insulation, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



*** There is an additional, specialized rebate program from Arctic Energy Alliance called  the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, accessble only until March 2024 that would allow you to double the rebate on the wall insulation. Please call me to let me know if you are seriously considering adding insulation to your home, so that we can discuss this program in more detail. ****



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.



This grant can be accessed in combination with any Arctic Energy Alliance grant.

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

WINDOW UPGRADE



All 6 windows in this house are older double paned, vinyl framed windows. Many are slider type windows that aren't necessarily in need of an upgrade, especially the large front window, but I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. If you do choose to upgrade your windows I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 190 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.0 GJ. Note, that the savings aren't huge given the cost of replacing the windows. Upgrading from slider to hinge type windows might help improve air leakage in your home.



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however there are rebates available from the Greener Homes Grant - Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details.



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</windows>
                <doors>

DOOR UPGRADE



The doors are older steel doors with extruded polystyrene insulation inside. Weather stripping was noted to be in need of repair, and the insulation beside the frames was visible during my evaluation, showing that it is currently fibreglass batt insulation. This could be upgraded to spray foam. There are currenlty no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.



The access door to your crawl space that houses the boiler and hot water heater was found to be uninsulated. You could add an inch or so of polystyrene insulation to the back of the door and some weather stripping to make this door have a higher R value. This door will not count for a rebate from Greener Homes.



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</doors>
                <HeatingSystem>

BOILER UPGRADE



One of the sources of cold air and discomfort found in your home was the combustion air supply pipe that is open, and constantly dumping cold, outside air into your mechanical room during the heating season. This is a typical installation style for older boilers, but by upgrading to a direct vent boiler, the combustion air, never interacts with air in the home. It eliminates that cold air source.



NATURAL GAS BOILER



Your house is currently heated with an 87% efficientl natural gas boiler. When it comes time to replace this boiler I have modeled the following boiler upgrade for you:



An ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing natural gas boiler with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



- This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Full program details are outlined on our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program.



This upgrade will eliminate the need for the current chimney and the open combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room. 



Savings:



By replacing your current natural gas boiler with a 97% AFUE condensing natural gas furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $400 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DOMESTIC HOT WATER



You described to me that you were not getting warm water from your new, on demand, condensing hot water tank, and after discussing this with a colleague it was suggested that you have a contractor come and look to make sure all pressure valves are working properly. Valves not working properly can cause the unti to not fire, resulting in no hot water.



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (your kitchen range hood does not vent outside, therefore the bathroom fan and clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.7 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current natural gas boiler and your older wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke detectors present, but I did not notice carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

       

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer

      

You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan because they seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



